    "question": {
      ":question/id": "962869b5-ede9-505b-ba42-b17dfe8b8305",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 2 x - 2 y + 2?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with both $x$ and $y$ is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186172040/plots/275cacf43d2b8460dca93bc1e89201126674d947f0f4356c40b65feffabda524.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line and a horizontal line, the slopes vary if we move along either of the two lines.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186172040/plots/9bfb7162a90d16df4ce1a6fed269e23005ec4a6bfa075853ee8e9847417ba700.png)",
